#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221184 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb9baafb-4eca-593f-ad3e-34744fdfd3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21684" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the global truncation error is ![](images/ee/ee7115883733062534307c8ff2b1795c77d47d1e8dffeb357dcbe94e07e1f400.png)\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the global truncation error is ![](images/38/3883232317dc9ab3e8888fab646fb593ca37585ef3367c5b3000be226962e512.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of ![](images/ea/ea99ff6ac18f5bb8506edcecfe913b338b64d04e99532f67cec6eb94efc2e9fa.png)\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n![](images/d8/d8eaeb8b6abf175f486560cf0d407fd97cd5180907c2db8e6436ccc7e4b09f04.png)" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =4(0.4)^{3}+(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.064+1\\cdot 0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.256+0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.281\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.282.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+1\\cdot 0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.032+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 2^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx  \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈8.380\\,952… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(8.380\\,95…) \\\\ p & ≈3.067….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221185 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "c94bf874-2055-5ff3-83e3-b5740a1e14e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21685" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.987… \\\\ p & ≈2.987….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221186 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/id #uuid "b9b0118d-5238-5644-a3d6-695d8f393efe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21686" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n\n- $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221187 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/id #uuid "de3c4be6-9d3d-5dcb-b1cf-567195c4ee63" :question/math-academy-id "e-21687" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24.$\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n![](images/31/31f454d50de05da419a55dfcdd26ac8d7883635e6eb0b95367f9f80fb30643a5.png)" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "7c30a36c-6dfd-538d-b000-0d232ead87f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220456}] :question/id #uuid "62f01af8-34f7-51b3-af20-ad432a77f51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333530" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2$.\nSuppose that using step size $Δx = 0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1) \\approx 0.072$.\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx = 0.05$, what GTE should we expect?$e(0.05) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecbe65b-19cc-51d7-8182-ccb6871927f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305759} {:answer/id #uuid "6f35542d-7224-54e0-9d5d-8f686b161502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306211} {:answer/id #uuid "c65a1b4b-0a47-5de4-92b7-c6771f8b1af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306996} {:answer/id #uuid "db2110b5-1300-5033-8663-510785a7cb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307181} {:answer/id #uuid "e1165ddc-eafe-5fed-94c5-61cc04c33c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1165ddc-eafe-5fed-94c5-61cc04c33c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307233} :answer-field/id #uuid "989a0b40-b3ed-53f1-afcd-b799ed9538cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3f43810-818f-5e05-945e-483e5e28efaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-333692" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3Δx.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is  $9(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2799aa5a-ed9b-5b21-bfd3-2dcb7524fe57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305523} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecc6880-8400-5596-b032-131ea2624d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306369} {:answer/id #uuid "828aa0c9-e28f-5245-9745-37d3d10f96f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306399} {:answer/id #uuid "a81c60b8-ab9f-5ed7-adec-427dc50c769d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306736} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b47cb7-81c5-5c0d-a157-33459420c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "828aa0c9-e28f-5245-9745-37d3d10f96f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306399} :answer-field/id #uuid "94522b9c-8ce8-51fb-835e-f8705f7e8843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f94b6c1-fa0e-5c48-970a-a0d605d1856d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334546" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516b9685-0ec7-5784-b050-aded8c8a1cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305943} {:answer/id #uuid "51732cc2-4655-5a0a-a764-680869a446ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305944} {:answer/id #uuid "6801fd43-32da-53c7-958f-9fd853108be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306143} {:answer/id #uuid "818a54c4-98fa-5ddd-8121-3a619a4286a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306389} {:answer/id #uuid "c22ebcd6-6d54-5211-b00c-21fc407fd31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c22ebcd6-6d54-5211-b00c-21fc407fd31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306962} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c894fd-10b5-50a4-85f2-09325e510c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9e60f41-0074-5df6-9afc-69a24a0bbeb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334644" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈50 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(50) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.269… \\\\ p & ≈3.269….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2fcee1-789f-5a65-b0c3-dbb43767d387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305422} {:answer/id #uuid "3223be5f-1c23-51da-ac5b-227bc9ab4e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305619} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9568f3-303b-5e68-b773-a6eb6264efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306339} {:answer/id #uuid "929c322b-6dd4-562d-bdcd-e14b9e67aee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306541} {:answer/id #uuid "b44f66d3-8c24-5e49-a0a1-36983570214c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c9568f3-303b-5e68-b773-a6eb6264efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306339} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4d4697-8b4c-5997-bfc4-9a5ec2b0f3a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f92b889e-ac1a-5f7b-b100-034485795a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-334650" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.20,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.20)=0.78.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.008.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.78$ to $0.008.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.78}{0.008}=97.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈97.5 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(97.5) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.99… \\\\ p & ≈3.99….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8cbf73-76e1-54a5-84d4-d80333a4ef75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305721} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdf10d7-6e1c-5467-b614-8a3266c84156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306607} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e8e5bc-4fba-5680-bb8e-d9821151b2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307083} {:answer/id #uuid "f2940528-333a-58e7-b7e9-e35fa963ea78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307386} {:answer/id #uuid "ff68c2e6-89a9-5261-a203-cad23dd679a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff68c2e6-89a9-5261-a203-cad23dd679a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307507} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7482acd-6813-50d9-954d-0ffe427a7d23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "512c526c-f958-5c94-ad6a-b60bd207799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334745" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c564be2-b5d6-576e-b6c5-4b2e22ea2310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c564be2-b5d6-576e-b6c5-4b2e22ea2310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305415} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f564b5-049c-5bd7-a511-bc72af30b334" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e9fb99c-0502-586a-b64c-76d5a0146e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334781" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.512.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.025,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.025)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.025.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.025}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}={4}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.025,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.025)≈\\frac{0.512}{256}=0.002.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6479670-90b2-527b-b1f2-f2ac3e1eca3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6479670-90b2-527b-b1f2-f2ac3e1eca3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306854} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab070b56-038b-599b-b680-8ef2d02605e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfc83ddc-db4f-5e46-adbe-84eb415a3d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334947" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.4)≈1.024.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.1}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={4}^{5}=1024.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.024}{1024}=0.001.\n$$"}]}
